@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: what one provider entry must declare, and the four places it must exist
' covers: internal/provider/**
' covers: internal/credentials/credentials.go
' covers: internal/credentials/auth_choice_test.go
title Provider registry — one entry, four obligations, three invariants

node "internal/provider" as PKG <<host>> {
  component "registry entry\nName · Detect · ACL · Hosts · Auth" as ENTRY <<app>>
  component "bareIDPrefixes\nmodel id → provider" as BARE <<app>>
  component "ambiguousBareIDs\nopen-weights: refuse to pin" as AMBIG <<app>>
  component "providerAliases\nupstream id → registry entry\n(opencode-go → opencode)" as ALIAS <<app>>
}

folder "The four places a provider must exist" as FOUR <<host>> {
  component "1. detection\nprovider.Detect(lookup)" as P1 <<app>>
  component "2. reach\nSquid provider-allow.conf" as P2 <<app>>
  component "3. pin\nModelProvider → broker" as P3 <<app>>
  component "4. harness\ncapabilities.providers" as P4 <<app>>
}

cloud "Harness public docs\nopencode · cursor · cecli" as DOCS <<cloud>>
component "Advertised but unregistered\n→ silent 401/403" as GHOST <<error>>

DOCS ARROW_CLOUD ENTRY : sync-providers skill
ENTRY ARROW_MAIN FOUR
BARE ARROW_MAIN P3
ALIAS ARROW_MAIN P3 : prefix folded before lookup
AMBIG ARROW_ERROR P3 : returns "" rather than guess
ENTRY ARROW_ERROR GHOST : any of the four missing

note bottom of ENTRY
  **An AuthOption can be OWNED by a harness.** ""Harness"" is empty for a
  general provider key any harness can send, and set where the credential is
  some CLI's own plan: ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"" (claudecode),
  ""CURSOR_API_KEY"" (cursor), ""OPENCODE_API_KEY"" (opencode).

  It exists because the auth row was offering them as spendable keys.
  ""CURSOR_API_KEY"" is set, real, and refused by every endpoint opencode can
  reach — cursor's API answers ""cursor-agent"" and nothing else, confirmed by
  Cursor staff on the forum: the CLI has no bring-your-own-key path. Detection
  and brokering still see the whole entry; only ""AuthVarsFor(name, harness)""
  filters, and only where the question is 'what can THIS run spend'.

  ""OPENCODE_API_KEY"" carries both plans of one gateway — models.dev lists
  env ['OPENCODE_API_KEY'] for opencode (Zen, pay-as-you-go) and for
  ""opencode-go"" (the subscription) alike, which is why the alias above folds
  to one route.
  SPEC: _spec/internal/credentials/credential-decisions.puml
end note

note bottom of ALIAS
  **One route, two upstream ids.** models.dev lists OpenCode's gateway as
  ""opencode"" (Zen, pay-as-you-go, ""/zen/v1"") and ""opencode-go"" (Go, the
  subscription, ""/zen/go/v1""), each with its own model list — but both read
  ""OPENCODE_API_KEY"" and both live on "".opencode.ai"". Detection, reach and
  injection see one provider, so the registry holds ONE entry and
  ""ModelProvider"" folds the ""opencode-go/"" prefix onto it; otherwise
  ""ARCHITECT_MODEL=opencode-go/…"" pinned a name ""Lookup"" could not find and
  ""MissingKeys"" named no credential for it.

  The key is a plain bearer opencode reads from its environment **ahead of**
  ""auth.json"", so the login is complete before launch — no ""/connect"" inside
  a sandbox whose ""auth.json"" the proveo home scrubs every run. opencode is
  not a ""subscription: true"" harness for that reason: nothing is minted
  inside, and the credential is detectable host-side like any other key.
end note

note right of ENTRY
  **A gap in any one of the four is silent.** The operator sets a key,
  nothing errors, and the agent receives the ""proveo-brokered"" sentinel —
  the provider then answers 401/403, which reads as a bad key rather
  than a proveo decision. That was the live failure that started this:
  a harness holding five keys could not pin, so every run got a
  sentinel.

  Two invariants are enforced by test, not by care:
  * every ""bareIDPrefixes"" entry resolves to a REGISTERED provider
  * every registered provider has an ACL and a detect key
    (bedrock/vertex exempt: cloud-SDK auth, no blanket ACL)

  A third is retired with the command that needed it: ""proveo init""
  no longer advertises keys, so it can no longer offer one the
  registry cannot use. It used to name DEEPSEEK/HUGGINGFACE/HF_TOKEN,
  none of them registered. See _spec/cmd/proveo/init-sbx-bootstrap.
end note

note bottom of AMBIG
  **An open-weights id names the model, not its host.** ""gpt-oss"",
  ""llama-*"", ""qwen*"", ""mixtral*"" are served by many providers, so
  claiming one for its originator routes the credential at the wrong
  vendor — a Cerebras run pinned ""openai"" until this existed. The
  vendor's own ids (""gpt-5"", ""grok-4"", ""glm-4.6"") still resolve; when a
  prefix is ambiguous the operator can always use ""provider/model"".
end note

note bottom of BARE
  **A local or shim prefix is not a provider.** ""ollama/"", ""ollama_chat/"" and
  ""openai-compatible/"" serve arbitrary ids from whatever endpoint the operator
  pointed them at, so ""ModelProvider"" returns "" — no pin, no route, and
  nothing to allowlist, because there is no vendor host to reach.

  That is a DIFFERENT silence from the ambiguous open-weights case. There the
  vendor is real and merely unknowable from the id, so ""provider/model"" is the
  operator's way out; here the prefix names a RUNTIME rather than a vendor, and
  there is nothing for a qualified id to name.
end note

note bottom of P2
  **Reach is not the pin.** The broker pins ONE provider because it
  injects one credential; the allowlist covers EVERY detected provider,
  because a session may switch model mid-run and narrowing reach to the
  injected provider would block it. This is a deliberate widening of
  egress surface for non-vendor-locked harnesses, accepted for that
  reason. Only a manifest ""provider:"" lock keeps the narrow allowlist.
end note

note bottom of DOCS
  **Scope: first-class only.** The registry carries 30 providers — those
  a harness names first-class in its own docs, or that directly vendor a
  model family an operator would put in ""ARCHITECT_MODEL"". OpenCode
  alone lists ~48 and cecli's LiteLLM surface many more; that long tail
  routes through ""openrouter"" or a custom OpenAI-compatible endpoint,
  because every individually allowlisted host is unvetted egress
  surface. Local runtimes (Ollama, LM Studio, llama\.cpp) are not
  providers at all — no egress to allowlist.

  Keep this reconciled with upstream via the ""sync-providers"" skill.
end note
note as AuthChoice #F4F9FB
  **Which credential, not just how it travels.** A provider may accept
  more than one — anthropic takes an API key OR a subscription OAuth
  token — and ""Resolve"" walked the declared ""Auth"" order and took the
  first one set. An operator holding both therefore ran on the API key
  with no warning and no way to say otherwise: the choice was an array
  index, not a decision.

  ""ResolveWith(name, preferVar, getenv)"" honours an explicit credential
  and falls back to the declared order when the preference is absent, so
  it never fails closed. The choice surfaces as an ""auth"" row in the
  prompt — offered ONLY when more than one credential is actually
  present — persists in ""agent-settings.yml"" as ""authVar"", and reaches
  the inspector as ""PROVEO_EGRESS_AUTH_VAR"". ""Resolved.EnvVar"" reports
  which one was used.

  ""--credentials broker|forward"" is a different axis: it governs HOW the
  credential travels, not WHICH one.
end note

note bottom of ENTRY
  **Detectability and broker-injectability are different properties.**
  Signed-request providers — Bedrock, Azure, Vertex — are detected and get
  a Squid write-pin ACL, but they have no static auth header to inject, so
  the broker cannot serve them. Resolution reports "not ok" for those
  rather than pretending, and a provider that accepts more than one
  credential (anthropic: an API key **or** a subscription OAuth token) is
  resolved against an explicit choice instead of a first-match guess.
end note

note bottom of AMBIG
  **A bare id names the model, not always who hosts it.** opencode and cecli
  are not vendor-locked, so an operator may write an unprefixed id and mean
  any of several hosts. Open-weights families are the ambiguous case —
  many providers serve them — so those ids are classified as ambiguous
  rather than mapped, and the pin is refused instead of guessed.
end note


@enduml
